1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,720
A manifest import throws ManifestException
— checked, on purpose, so callers must
handle bad customs data. The service
method is @Transactional. The exception
escapes. The transaction commits anyway.

2
00:00:14,839 --> 00:00:29,039
Half a manifest sits in the database with
a status that says “accepted.” Nightly
reconciliation fails. Someone asks why
Spring “ignored” the error. It did not
ignore the error. It followed the default
rollback rules.

3
00:00:29,159 --> 00:00:38,879
Unchecked exceptions — RuntimeException
and subclasses — and Error mark the
transaction for rollback. Checked
exceptions do not, unless you list them.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,519
The interceptor inspects what escapes the
advised method, matches rollback and no-
rollback rules, and tells the manager to
commit or roll back.

5
00:00:46,640 --> 00:01:01,759
For harbor manifests, the business meaning
is usually clear: a checked
ManifestException is fatal to the unit of
work. Say so on the annotation. Look at
this java for a moment. Look at this java
for a moment.

6
00:01:01,879 --> 00:01:09,399
Read the attributes aloud. rollbackFor =
ManifestException.class means: even though
this is checked, undo the transaction.

7
00:01:09,519 --> 00:01:14,840
Without it, the default would commit the
saved manifest after a customs reject —
the bug that started this episode.

8
00:01:14,959 --> 00:01:27,399
noRollbackFor =
CustomsLinkDownException.class means: even
though this is a runtime exception, keep
the intake; mark notify-pending and let a
retry path finish the side effect.

9
00:01:27,519 --> 00:01:35,840
Defaults would have wiped a valid manifest
because a downstream link blinked. Rules
match the exception that actually escapes,
including subclasses.

10
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:46,839
Catch inside the method and swallow
without rethrowing, and the interceptor
sees success — partial state may commit.
Catching is not a rollback rule; it is a
decision that the boundary completed.

11
00:01:46,959 --> 00:02:02,639
You can list multiple types, or use the
stringly rollbackForClassName forms in
older configs. Prefer explicit class
literals. In tests, @Rollback / @Commit
steer the test transaction — related idea,
different switch.

12
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:15,519
A frequent bug is wrapping a runtime
failure in a checked type, adding throws,
and forgetting rollbackFor. Another is
noRollbackFor = Exception.class, then
wondering why serious failures still
commit.

13
00:02:15,639 --> 00:02:28,959
Prefer domain exceptions with clear fatal
versus recoverable meaning, and align the
annotation with that meaning — especially
for checked ManifestException on intake
paths. Local rules still assume one
resource manager.

14
00:02:29,079 --> 00:02:40,919
The day intake must write Postgres and
publish a JMS booking message in one
breath, a single local boundary is no
longer enough. Distributed transactions
are where that stretch gets honest — and
expensive.
